-# --- line continuations -----------------------------------------------------
+# --- where a `#` starts a comment --------------------------------------------
+# Bash starts a comment at a `#` that begins a word in a COMMAND context, and the walkers cannot
+# ask bash which those are, so this is bash's rule measured context by context (bash 5.2) and
+# written down once, for the two readers that act on a comment: `join_continuations`, which
+# drops comment text before any walker reads the command, and `heredoc_delim`, which must not
+# open a body on a `<<` inside one. Two readers with two rules is how one shape gets judged as
+# a comment by one and as a command by the other (AMH ledger row DD055).
+#
+# In a command context a `#` starts a comment after a blank, a newline, `;`, `&` or `|`; after
+# an OPENING backtick, and inside backticks the comment ends at the closing one
+# (`` `#x`; cmd `` runs `cmd`); after the `(` of a subshell, `$(`, `<(` or `>(`; and after a
+# `)` that closes a subshell, `((…))` or a case pattern. It does NOT after `=`, `]`, `}`, a
+# word character or a CLOSING backtick, nor after the `)` that closes `$(`, `<(`, `>(` or
+# `$((`: those end a word, so `echo $(date)#x; cmd` runs `cmd`. After `<` or `>` bash does
+# start one, which leaves the redirection without a target, so bash refuses the whole line and
+# runs nothing; the verdict there is no, which keeps the text to judge at no cost.
+#
+# Some contexts take no comment at all, whatever comes before the `#`: arithmetic
+# (`((1 #x)); cmd` runs `cmd`), an extglob or other word-level paren (`@(a #b)`), the parens
+# of `[[ … ]]` (`[[ x =~ (#x) ]]`), and `${…}`. So the walk keeps a stack of open contexts,
+# innermost last, one letter each. The command contexts: c a subshell, s a command or process
+# substitution, t a backtick substitution, g a `{ …; }` group, j the arm of a `case`, and r an
+# array's `=(`, which takes comments as they do. A `case` is k while it reads patterns — from
+# the keyword to a pattern's `)`, and again after `;;`, `;&` or `;;&` — and j in each arm; a
+# `(` that opens a pattern is q. The others: a arithmetic (the inner `(` of `((` or `$((`, and
+# every plain `(` inside one), w any other word-level paren, and p a `${`, in which a plain `(`
+# opens nothing. A `$(`, `<(` or `>(` opens a command context anywhere; any other `(` opens a
+# subshell only where a command starts (see `comment_cmdpos`), so a function's `f(`, an
+# extglob's `@(` and the parens of `[[` are w, while `until(` and `if (` are subshells. A `)`
+# closes only a paren and a `}` only a brace: `${x:-)}` holds a literal `)`, and bash's `${`
+# ends at its first `}`. Bash parses a backtick substitution on its own, so a closing backtick
+# drops every context opened since its opening one.
+#
+# Three answers, not two. Where the walker cannot tell, the verdict is UNKNOWN and each reader
+# takes the reading that judges more text: `join_continuations` keeps the text, `heredoc_delim`
+# opens no body. Unknown covers a `#` after the `)` of a, w or r (bash comments after `f()` but
+# not after `@(…)` or `a=(…)`), after a `)` with nothing to close, and after a `)` whose `(` a
+# `case` may have thrown off. k is pushed only for a `case` keyword in command position, and in
+# a pattern list `case` and `esac` are pattern words, since a k pushed for a mere word would
+# read the `)` of `$(echo x; case a)` as a pattern's. Any other `case` word — after `f()`, say —
+# marks every open context uncertain (upper case): its pattern `)` may then pop the wrong one,
+# and `( echo $(f() case a in a) x;; esac; f)#y; cmd )` must not read `#y; cmd )` as a comment.
+# Such a `case` met inside a, w, p or r makes every later yes an unknown, since a wrong pop
+# there could hide that the `#` is still in arithmetic. A word that only looks like `case`
+# costs an unknown, never a dropped command.
+COMMENT_STACK=''   # one letter per open context, innermost last; see above
+COMMENT_CLOSED=''  # the letter the latest `)` closed; empty when it closed nothing
+COMMENT_BLIND=0    # 1 once a `case` was met inside arithmetic, a word paren, `${` or `=(`
+COMMENT_TEXT=''    # the text being walked, for comment_word's look back
+COMMENT_VERDICT='' # yes, no or unknown, set by comment_verdict
+comment_reset() { # comment_reset <the text about to be walked>
+	COMMENT_STACK=''
+	COMMENT_CLOSED=''
+	COMMENT_BLIND=0
+	COMMENT_TEXT=$1
+}
